{0 How to Drive [odoc]}

[odoc] is a command-line tool. It turns the compiled modules and the [.mld]
pages of a project into documentation, one file at a time, through several
distinct phases. Like a compiler, it needs something to call it in the right
order with the right arguments. That something is a {e driver}.

The {{!/odoc-driver/page-index}odoc-driver} package holds the {e reference
driver}, which is kept in step with [odoc]. Other drivers exist, such as
{{:https://dune.readthedocs.io/en/stable/documentation.html}dune} and
{{!/odig/page-index}odig}.

This document is for anyone who calls [odoc] themselves: writing a driver
over a switch full of other people's packages, or arranging for a project to
document its own libraries. Those are the same job with different sources of
information. What has to be known before anything can be compiled is the same
either way, and {{!installed}step 1} sets it out; everything after it is
identical. You do not need this document to {e use} [odoc] through a driver
someone else wrote. It covers:

- the model [odoc] uses to organise documentation: {{!units}trees};
- the four {{!pipeline}phases} and the command line of each;
- a {{!walkthrough}walkthrough} of what the reference driver does for one
  package, then what changes for {{!other}other kinds of packages} and for
  {{!ocamlorg}ocaml.org};
- the {{!conv}convention} that drivers and installed packages follow so that
  they work together.

The walkthrough names the modules of the reference driver that carry out each
step. Reading it next to the {{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib}library's own
documentation} is a good way to understand the whole process.

{1:units Trees of documentation}

The same documentation must work in several settings: a local switch, a large
monorepo, or the {{:https://ocaml.org/packages}ocaml.org} hub for all
packages. In each setting the pages live at different places, and references
between them must still resolve.

[odoc] solves this with named groups of documentation, called {e trees}.
There are two kinds: {e page trees} and {e module trees}. [odoc] manages the
hierarchy inside a tree. The driver decides where each tree is rooted in the
overall hierarchy. A documentation author refers to another tree by its name,
not by its location.

Concretely, a tree is a name, a root {{!conv}identifier}, and a directory of
[.odoc] files. The driver passes the two kinds as [-P name:dir] and
[-L name:dir], the name is what an author writes in [{!/name/...}], and the
files below the directory are laid out to match the identifiers below the
root. The reference driver makes one page tree per package and one module
tree per library; nothing obliges a driver to do the same.

For opam packages the trees have a natural meaning. Each package has a page
tree named after the package. Each of its libraries has a module tree named
after the library. Another package can refer to the pages by the package name,
and to a module by the library name, wherever the package ends up in the
hierarchy. The {{!conv}convention} below spells this out.

{1:pipeline The pipeline}

Documentation is generated in four phases. A file may refer to a module that
depends on it, so the phases are separated to break this apparent cycle.

- The {e compile} phase turns [.cmi], [.cmt], [.cmti] and [.mld] files into
  [.odoc] files. Here [odoc] does work similar to the compiler's, such as
  computing the expansion of each module type. The dependencies between
  inputs are those of the compiled modules themselves.

- The {e link} phase turns [.odoc] files into [.odocl] files. It resolves the
  references written in documentation comments, and canonical paths.

- The {e indexing} phase gathers [.odocl] files into [.odoc-index] files, from
  which the sidebar and the search index are made.

- The {e generation} phase renders [.odocl] files, with the sidebar made in
  the phase before, as HTML, Markdown, man pages or LaTeX.

Three binaries are involved, and a driver calls all three. [odoc] itself does
everything above. [odoc-md] turns a Markdown page into an [.odoc] file, since
[odoc compile] reads only [.mld]. [sherlodoc] builds the search database that
the generated pages query, and ships the JavaScript that queries it; a driver
that wants no search need not call it.

{2 The compile phase}

There is one command per kind of input: [odoc compile] for interfaces and
pages, [odoc compile-impl] for implementations, [odoc compile-asset] for
assets.

{3 Compiling interfaces and pages}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile --parent-id <pid> -o <file>.odoc -L <lib1>:<dir1> -L <lib2>:<dir2> <input>
]}

- [<input>] is a [.cmi], [.cmt], [.cmti] or [.mld] file. Prefer [.cmti] files
  over the other compiled forms: they hold the documentation of the [.mli].

- [--parent-id <pid>] places the unit in the documentation hierarchy. It is a
  path of non-empty names separated by [/]. Together with the input's name it
  gives the unit its {e identifier}, which decides how references to the unit
  resolve and where its HTML goes. Units of one {{!units}tree} share a common
  prefix of their parent ids. The identifier is stored inside the [.odoc] file.

- [-o <file>.odoc] is where the file goes. The name must be the input's name
  with the [.odoc] extension, prefixed by [page-] for an [.mld]: [odoc] finds
  a unit by that name, capitalised, and the prefix tells it what kind of unit
  to expect. Without [-o], [odoc] writes the file below [--output-dir] at the
  path given by [--parent-id].

  A tree's files may be rooted wherever the driver likes, since later phases
  are handed the directory. Within a tree they are not free: a path reference
  such as [{!/pkg/a/b/page-c}] is resolved by looking for [a/b/page-c.odoc]
  below the directory that [-P pkg:] names, so a unit's path below that
  directory must match its identifier below the tree's root. A [{!//a/b}]
  reference is resolved relative to the input file's own directory.

- [-L <name>:<dir>] names a library and the directory of its [.odoc] files.
  The directory is searched for the modules the input depends on, those
  [odoc compile-deps] lists for it, so each directory the compiler searched
  for [.cmi] files when it built the input should have a counterpart here.
  Several files of the same name may be on the search path; [odoc] picks the
  one whose interface digest matches.

  The names are written into the [.odoc] file. They are the libraries the
  compiler could see when the unit was built, and [odoc link] uses them later:
  resolution that follows a path or a reference {e into} this unit carries on
  among these libraries, so a module named inside it is the one it was built
  against. Without that, two libraries built against different modules of the
  same name are indistinguishable from the outside.

- [-I <dir>] does the same without naming a library, and so records nothing.
  It is what drivers passed before [-L] existed, and is deprecated for these
  two commands.

For example, for the [fmt.cli] library the reference driver runs:

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile ~/.opam/5.5.0/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.cmti
      -o _odoc/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.odoc
      -L fmt.cli:_odoc/lib/fmt/cli/ -L fmt:_odoc/lib/fmt/
      -L cmdliner:_odoc/lib/cmdliner/ -L stdlib:_odoc/lib/ocaml/
      --parent-id fmt/fmt.cli --warnings-tag fmt --enable-missing-root-warning
]}

{3 Compiling implementations}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-impl --parent-id <pid> --source-id <sid> -o impl-<file>.odoc -L <lib1>:<dir1> <input>.cmt
]}

The input is a [.cmt] file. The output name is [impl-] followed by the input's
name. [--parent-id] and [-L] mean the same as above, the names being written
into the unit in the same way; the implementation is usually given the same
parent id as its interface. [--source-id <sid>] is the
identifier of the page that will show the rendered source; links to the source
point there.

{3 Compiling Markdown pages}

{@shell[
  $ odoc-md <file>.md -o <file>.odoc --parent-id <pid>
]}

[odoc compile] reads [.mld] and compiled interfaces, not Markdown, so a
Markdown page goes through the separate [odoc-md] binary. [--parent-id] and
[-o] mean what they do for [odoc compile], and [--output-dir] may be given
instead of [-o], with the same meaning. Everything after this phase treats
the result as any other page.

{3 Compiling assets}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-asset -o asset-<file>.odoc --parent-id <pid> --name <file>
]}

An asset is copied at generation time, but it needs a unit so that references
to it resolve. [-o] and [--output-dir] mean what they do for [odoc compile].

{2 The link phase}

{@shell[
  $ odoc link <file>.odoc -o <file>.odocl
      -P <pkg1>:<dir1> -P <pkg2>:<dir2>
      -L <lib1>:<dir3> -L <lib2>:<dir4>
      -I <dir5> -I <dir6>
]}

- [-P <name>:<dir>] names a page tree and the directory of its [.odoc] files.
  References such as [{!/ocamlfind/index}] resolve against it.

- [-L <name>:<dir>] names a module tree likewise. References such as
  [{!/findlib.dynload/Fl_dynload}] resolve against it, and references by
  plain name search its directory.

- [-I <dir>] adds a directory to the search path.

Odoc looks up a module by name in these directories, but not all at once, and
what it tries first depends on whose name it is resolving.

A name odoc holds a digest for is matched by that digest, anywhere on the
search path, and the order below does not arise. That covers most of what a
signature mentions, since the compiler recorded those modules as imports.

For the rest, the canonical paths and the references written in documentation
comments, odoc searches in three steps while it resolves the unit named on the
command line. First the libraries that unit recorded when it was compiled,
each in the directory its [-L] names. Then the [-I] directories. Only then the
[-L] directories as a whole. The first two hold what the compiler gave that
unit, so a name they answer means the module it was built against.

When resolution follows a path or a reference {e into} another unit, it
carries on among the libraries that unit recorded at compile time, which are
what its own compiler could see. This is what lets a page name a submodule of
one library rather than of its sibling, and lets a module list show each
module's own first paragraph resolved as that module's page resolves it.

A module of a library that is in neither is found by the wider search, which
has no digest to tell same-named candidates apart: odoc takes the first and
warns. Authors can avoid the ambiguity with a path reference that names the
tree, [{!/foo/A}] for the module [A] of library [foo].

No two directories given to [-P] may be nested or shared, and likewise for
[-L]; the two sets are checked separately, so a [-P] directory may contain a
[-L] one. Where even that is impossible, several libraries installed in one
directory for instance, pass [--custom-layout], which disables the check.

A name that the unit's imports record with a digest is matched by that digest,
anywhere on the search path. Every other name is looked up among the
libraries the unit recorded when it was compiled, each in the directory its
[-L] names. Those are what the compiler gave the unit.

What happens when they do not answer depends on the name. A path is a
canonical path, an alias, or anything else the compiler wrote. It can only
mean a module the compiler gave the unit, so it is reported as missing. It is
not answered by a same-named module of another library. A reference written
in a documentation comment may name anything in the reference scope, which is
wider. So a reference is looked up along the whole search path as well. Where
two libraries there define the same module name, [odoc] takes the first and
warns.

A page records no libraries, and nor does a module compiled with [-I] alone.
Such a unit looks its names up along the whole path from the start. An author
who needs to name a module of a particular library writes a path reference,
[{!/libname/Module}].

Implementations are looked up along [-I] alone, never [-L]. That is what
turns an identifier in a rendered source file into a link to the source of
the module it came from. A driver therefore passes [-I] at link as well.

{2 The indexing phase}

{3 Counting occurrences}

{@shell[
  $ odoc count-occurrences <dir1> <dir2> -o <file>.odoc-occurrences
]}

This counts how often each item is used by the implementations found below the
directories. The counts are used only by the JSON search index below.

{3 Indexing entries}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-index -o <file>.odoc-index --file-list <list> [--occurrences <file>]
]}

[<list>] names a file with one [.odocl] path per line. [odoc] builds one
hierarchy from these units' identifiers. Directories can be given instead with
[--root <dir>], but each root then gives a hierarchy of its own, which is
wrong when a package's pages and modules live in different directories. The
binary index does not use occurrence counts; only the JSON form does, with
[--json].

{@shell[
  $ odoc sidebar-generate -o <file>.odoc-sidebar <file>.odoc-index
]}

This derives the sidebar from an index. With [--json] it writes the JSON form
used by ocaml.org.

{3 The search database}

{@shell[
  $ sherlodoc index --format js <file>.odoc-index -o <dir>/sherlodoc_db.js
  $ sherlodoc js <dir>/sherlodoc.js
]}

[sherlodoc] is a separate binary. The first command turns the index into the
database a page queries, the second writes the engine that queries it, which
every page shares. Both paths are then given to [odoc html-generate] as
[--search-uri], relative to the HTML directory. [--format marshal] writes the
database a server searches instead, and [--favoured_prefixes] lists module
prefixes whose results come first.

{2 The generation phase}

{@shell[
  $ odoc html-generate <file>.odocl -o <html-dir>
      --sidebar <file>.odoc-sidebar
      --search-uri <path.js> --search-uri <path2.js>
]}

The output goes below [<html-dir>] at the path given by the unit's identifier:
a directory named after a module, a file named after a page. [--sidebar] gives
the sidebar to show. Each [--search-uri] is a JavaScript file, relative to the
HTML directory, that the page loads in a web worker to answer search queries.
The worker receives the query as a string and replies with a string holding a
JSON list of objects with a [url] and an [html] field.

{@shell[
  $ odoc html-generate-source --impl impl-<file>.odocl <source>.ml -o <html-dir>
  $ odoc html-generate-asset --asset-unit asset-<file>.odoc <file> -o <html-dir>
  $ odoc support-files -o <html-dir>
]}

These render a source file, copy an asset to its place, and write the
stylesheets and scripts every page needs.

{1:walkthrough What the reference driver does}

This section follows the reference driver through one ordinary package, then
looks at the cases that need something more. The driver is the
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib}[Odoc_driver_lib]} library, with two
executables on top: [odoc_driver] for a switch, [odoc_driver_voodoo] for
ocaml.org.

{2:standard A standard package: [fmt]}

The [fmt] package installs these files in the switch:

{v
lib/fmt/META
lib/fmt/fmt.cmi  fmt.cmti  fmt.cmt  fmt.mli  fmt.ml  fmt.cma ...
lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.cmi  fmt_cli.cmti  fmt_cli.cmt  fmt_cli.cma ...
lib/fmt/tty/fmt_tty.cmi  fmt_tty.cmti  fmt_tty.cmt  fmt_tty.cma ...
doc/fmt/odoc-pages/index.mld
doc/fmt/README.md  CHANGES.md  LICENSE.md
v}

The [META] file defines three libraries. [fmt] requires nothing; [fmt.cli]
requires [cmdliner] and [fmt]; [fmt.tty] requires [unix] and [fmt].

Running [odoc_driver fmt] documents it and the packages it depends on.

{3:installed Step 1: finding out what is installed}

Four things have to be known before anything is compiled, and they are the
same four whether the modules belong to other people's packages or to the
project in front of you:

- {b What the modules are:} for each one, a compiled interface to read. A
  [.cmti] is best, since it carries the documentation written in the [.mli];
  failing that a [.cmt] or a [.cmi].

- {b How the modules are grouped into libraries.} A module is named within a
  library rather than within the world: two libraries may each hold a module
  [Content], and which one a reference means is settled by knowing the library
  the referring module belongs to. The grouping is not presentation, it is
  what makes the names unambiguous.

- {b What depends on what,} at both levels. A library's dependencies are the
  libraries whose modules it may name, and they fix the order the libraries
  are compiled in; a module's dependencies fix the order within a library.

- {b What else belongs to the package:} its name, its [.mld] pages, its
  Markdown, its assets, its source files, and its [odoc-config.sexp] if it has
  one.

None of that is specific to opam or to findlib. A project that documents
itself knows all four already, because its build system had to know them to
build the code at all, and it is free to state them however it likes — a
[dune] rule, a list written by hand. The flags described in
{{!pipeline}the phases above} are the whole of the interface to [odoc]. The
rest of this step is how the reference driver recovers the same four facts
for a switch of packages it did not build, which is the awkward case rather
than the general one.

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Packages}[Packages]} asks opam which packages
[fmt] depends on and which files each package installed, and asks findlib
({{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Ocamlfind}[Ocamlfind]}) about every library:
its directory, its archives and its [requires]. From that it builds one
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Packages.t}[Packages.t]} per package. For
[fmt] this holds three libraries, each with the modules of its archive found by
[odoc classify], and each module analysed with [odoc compile-deps] to get the
digest of its interface and the modules it depends on. It also holds the
page [index.mld], the Markdown files, and the contents of [odoc-config.sexp]
if the package has one. Those two [odoc] commands exist for this situation:
they answer the grouping and the dependency questions from installed files
alone, when there is nothing better to go on. A driver that is handed the
answers has no need of them.

Nor can a driver assume findlib is there to be asked. It is configured by
[ocamlfind], which is a package like any other, so a switch holding only the
closure of a package built with dune need not contain it, and ocaml.org
documents packages in exactly such switches. The two facts findlib would have
supplied — a library's [requires], and its object directory relative to the
switch, [lib/fmt/cli] for [fmt.cli], which is the directory the later steps
mirror — are then read from what is on disk. Every installed package ships
the [META] that names its libraries, their directories and their [requires],
and reading one needs no configuration. The compiler's own libraries ship no
[META] of their own, but they are below the directory [ocamlc -where] prints,
one subdirectory each, and a sub-library shares its parent's:
[compiler-libs.common] is in [compiler-libs], [threads.posix] in [threads]. A
library nothing says anything about is still compiled against the standard
library, and assuming that is what lets its modules resolve the signatures
they are constrained by.

{3 Step 2: deciding identifiers and locations}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Odoc_units_of}[Odoc_units_of]} turns each
[Packages.t] into an {{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Odoc_unit.pkg}[Odoc_unit.pkg]}:
the list of units to build, grouped by library, with everything the later
steps need.

Each unit gets an {e identifier} and a {e location}. They follow different
layouts, and keeping them apart is the point.

The identifier follows the {{!conv}convention}: the package's pages under the
package name, a library's modules under [<pkg>/<lib>]. It decides the URLs.

{v
fmt/page-index          the package's index page, from doc/fmt/odoc-pages/index.mld
fmt/fmt/Fmt             the module Fmt of library fmt
fmt/fmt.cli/Fmt_cli     the module Fmt_cli of library fmt.cli
fmt/fmt/page-index      the library's landing page, written by the driver
v}

The location mirrors the switch. A library's [.odoc] files are written at the
path of its object directory, and a package's pages under [doc/<pkg>]:

{v
_odoc/lib/fmt/fmt.odoc            beside lib/fmt/fmt.cmi in the switch
_odoc/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.odoc
_odoc/doc/fmt/page-index.odoc     the package's index page
_odoc/doc/fmt/fmt/page-index.odoc the landing page of library fmt
v}

The same paths hold under the directory for [.odocl] files. See
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Odoc_unit}[Odoc_unit]} for the functions that
compute both layouts.

Mirroring the switch has two consequences. Libraries installed in one
directory share one odoc directory, so a single [-I] finds all of them, exactly
as a single [-I] found their [.cmi] files. And every location is a function of
a name: a library's from its findlib directory, a package's from its name. A
run that documents a package on its own can therefore find what earlier runs
built without any record of it. This is how ocaml.org works, see
{{!ocamlorg}below}. The odoc directory may even be the switch prefix itself.

{3:scope Step 3: the search path and the scope}

For each library, [Odoc_units_of] computes its {e dependency cone}: the
library itself and, transitively, what it requires, each with the directory of
its [.odoc] files. This is what the compiler searched when it built the
library. For [fmt.cli] it is [fmt.cli], [fmt], [cmdliner] and the standard
library. The cone is given to [odoc compile] with [-L], which both finds the
files and writes the names into the units, and to [odoc link] with [-I].

For each package it computes the {e reference scope}: the [-P] and [-L]
arguments its units are linked with. Every unit of the package gets the same
scope. It is not the transitive closure:

- [-L]: the package's own libraries, the libraries they require directly, and
  the libraries named in [odoc-config.sexp];
- [-P]: the package itself, the packages providing those libraries, and the
  packages named in [odoc-config.sexp].

The page the driver writes for a library is linked with that library's [-I]
as well, although it is a page and has no modules of its own. It shows a
synopsis for each module, and needs to reach what those synopses name.

For [fmt], with [cmdliner] not installed as a dependency, the link command of
[Fmt] is:

{@shell[
  $ odoc link _odoc/lib/fmt/fmt.odoc -o _odocl/lib/fmt/fmt.odocl
      -P fmt:_odoc/doc/fmt -P ocaml-compiler:_odoc/doc/ocaml-compiler
      -L fmt:_odoc/lib/fmt/ -L fmt.cli:_odoc/lib/fmt/cli/ -L fmt.tty:_odoc/lib/fmt/tty/
      -L stdlib:_odoc/lib/ocaml/ -L unix:_odoc/lib/ocaml/unix/
      -I _odoc/lib/fmt/ -I _odoc/lib/ocaml/
      --current-package fmt --warnings-tags fmt --custom-layout --enable-missing-root-warning
]}

Which package provides a library comes from opam's record of installed files:
[stdlib] and [unix] are in [lib/ocaml], installed by [ocaml-compiler]. A
library stands in this list only if it is being built in this run or its
[.odoc] directory exists already; otherwise the libraries it requires stand in
for it. That is what handles an alias, [num] requiring only [num.core] or
[threads.posix] requiring only [threads], and it is also what drops a library
nobody has documented, here [cmdliner], which then contributes nothing.

{3 Step 4: compiling}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Build}[Build]} drives the whole run, package
by package, in the order the packages were found. Each one goes through
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Build.compile_package}[Build.compile_package]},
which compiles its libraries with
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.compile_lib}[Compile.compile_lib]}
and its pages with
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.compile_pages}[Compile.compile_pages]}.
That function is the whole of the compile phase for one package, and it is
what {{!ocamlorg}a voodoo job} calls too.

Order matters twice, and it is a requirement rather than an optimisation:
[odoc compile] computes each module's expansions, so a dependency that has not
been compiled yet is one whose signature cannot be expanded, and no later
phase repairs that. Within a library, a module is compiled after the modules
it depends on, so that their [.odoc] files exist when [odoc] looks for them.
Between libraries, a library is compiled after the libraries it requires:
[fmt.cli] after [fmt], [fmt] after [stdlib]. [Build] keeps a table of the
libraries compiled so far and compiles a library's requirements first,
recursively. Requiring is a relation between libraries, not packages, and no
library requires itself even indirectly, so this always ends.

The compile command for [fmt_cli.cmti] was shown {{!pipeline}above}. Its [-L]
arguments name the four libraries of the cone with their directories.
[lib/fmt/fmt.odoc] is already there because [fmt] was compiled first, and
[lib/ocaml/stdlib.odoc] because [ocaml-compiler] was an earlier package. The
names go into [fmt_cli.odoc], so a later run resolving something into that
module knows the four libraries it may look in.

{3 Step 5: linking}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.link}[Compile.link]} links every unit
of the package with the package's scope, and each library's units with its
[-I] path as well, its page included. Linking needs the [.odoc] files of
everything the scope names, so [Build] makes sure each package in the scope
has been compiled before it links. For [fmt] that is already so. When it is
not, see the {{!forward}forward references} below.

{3 Step 6: index, sidebar, search and HTML}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.generate}[Compile.generate]} finishes
the package. It writes the list of the package's [.odocl] files and runs
[odoc compile-index] on it, so that pages and modules, though in different
directories, form one hierarchy. From the index it makes the sidebar with
[odoc sidebar-generate] and the search database with [sherlodoc]. Then it
runs [odoc html-generate] on each unit, and [odoc html-generate-source] on
each implementation whose source was found. The output:

{v
_html/fmt/index.html                the package page
_html/fmt/fmt/index.html            the library landing page
_html/fmt/fmt/Fmt/index.html        the module
_html/fmt/src/fmt/fmt.ml.html       its rendered source
_html/fmt/sidebar.json              the sidebar, for ocaml.org
_html/fmt/sherlodoc_db.js           the search database
_html/index.html                    the list of documented packages
v}

Warnings [odoc] printed while compiling and linking [fmt] are shown at the end
of the run. Those of dependency packages are not: the driver is for fixing the
documentation of the packages you asked for.

{2:other Other kinds of packages}

{3 Several libraries in one directory}

The compiler's libraries [compiler-libs.common], [compiler-libs.optcomp] and
the others all live in [lib/ocaml/compiler-libs]. A module of one may use a
module of another without the [META] file saying so; the compiler never
noticed because one [-I] covered them all. Mirroring the switch gives the
driver the same behaviour: their [.odoc] files share
[_odoc/lib/ocaml/compiler-libs], and one [-I] finds all of them. Their [-L]
roots then name the same directory, which is why the driver always passes
[--custom-layout].

{3 Virtual libraries}

[checkseum] is a virtual library with two implementations, [checkseum.c] and
[checkseum.ocaml]. An implementation ships its modules as [.cmt] files only;
the [.mli], and the documentation in it, belong to the virtual library, which
ships the [.cmti]. Both have the same interface digest.

The driver documents each implementation by compiling the virtual library's
[.cmti] again, under the implementation's own parent id:
[checkseum.cmti] is compiled three times, under [checkseum/checkseum],
[checkseum/checkseum.c] and [checkseum/checkseum.ocaml].
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Packages.remap_virtual}[Packages.remap_virtual]}
makes the substitution when the virtual library is part of the run. When it
was built by an earlier run, as on ocaml.org, [Compile.compile_lib] looks for
a same-named [.cmti] with the right digest along the implementation's [-I]
path; the driver copies the [.cmti] next to the virtual library's [.odoc] file
for this purpose.

The package's landing pages list each library's modules with path references,
[{!modules: /checkseum.c/Checkseum}], because three modules named [Checkseum]
are in scope and a plain [{!Checkseum}] would pick one at random.

{3:siblings Libraries that share module names}

A package may ship two libraries that define the same module names on purpose.
[eliom] is the example: [eliom.client] and [eliom.server] mirror each other,
and that symmetry is the point of the framework. Nothing in the reference scope
tells them apart, so a plain [{!Eliom_content}] in a doc comment is ambiguous:
odoc warns and takes the first it finds. An author who means one of them writes
a path reference, [{!/eliom.client/Eliom_content}].

A module's own documentation is not affected. Its [-I] holds its library's
dependency cone and never both siblings, and odoc searches that first, so the
types it shows, the modules its signature mentions and the references its
comments make all stay on the side they were written on.

Nor is what a page of the package says about those modules, although a page
has no cone of its own. A reference into one of them, [{!/eliom.client/
Eliom_content.Html}], and the first paragraph a module list shows beside each
entry, both resolve among the libraries that module recorded when it was
compiled. What remains ambiguous is only a name that says nothing about where
it comes from, the plain [{!Eliom_content}] above.

{3 Alias libraries}

Some findlib packages provide no modules and exist only to require others:
[num] requires [num.core], [threads.posix] requires [threads],
[compiler-libs] requires [compiler-libs.common]. A [META] file naming one of
these means the libraries it requires. The driver follows the requirement when
computing the scope and the compile order, so that [sexplib.num], which
requires [num], gets [-L num.core] and is compiled after it.

{3 Archives that hold other archives}

A module can be in more than one archive, and [odoc classify] says so, because
it is the truth: the OCaml distribution ships its middle end both as
[ocamlmiddleend.cma], of 77 modules, and inside [ocamloptcomp.cma], which
holds those 77 and 49 more. A module is documented once, so the driver gives
each one to the largest library that holds it. The bundling archive is the one
that ships as a library: findlib declares [compiler-libs.optcomp] and has no
name at all for the middle end, so that is also the library a reader can
depend on. A library left with nothing of its own is not documented, and the
driver says which.

This only arises where libraries are named after their archives, which is what
the driver falls back to when a package installs no [META]. Where the [META]
is there, the archives it does not name are not libraries and the question
does not come up.

{3 Libraries that were not built}

A [META] names the libraries a package can install, not the ones it did. A
package built without an optional dependency still declares the sub-library it
skipped: [fmt] declares [fmt.cli] when [cmdliner] was absent, [logs] declares
[logs.lwt], [logs.fmt] and the rest. The directory the [META] names is then not
there, and the driver documents nothing for that library and logs that it
found nothing. The same happens for a [META] that points outside its own
package, as [topkg] points at [topkg-care].

This is worth knowing when comparing two runs: a library missing from the
output may mean the package was built differently, not that the driver
dropped it.

{3:forward Forward references through [odoc-config.sexp]}

A package's pages may refer to a package built on top of it. [eio]'s
documentation points at [Eio_main.run], and [eio_main] depends on [eio].
[eio] declares this in [doc/eio/odoc-config.sexp]:

{v
(packages eio_main bigstring)
(libraries unix)
v}

This adds [eio_main]'s pages and libraries to [eio]'s scope. But [eio_main]
comes later in the build, so its [.odoc] files do not exist when [eio] would
be linked. [Build] handles this by compiling first and linking later: before
it links a package, it compiles every package the scope names. So it compiles
[eio], then [eio_main], then links [eio], and later links [eio_main] in its
turn. Compiling never needs the forward reference, since it follows
[requires] only, and linking only needs the [.odoc] files. This is the same
split ocaml.org makes between the two phases, see below.

{3 Packages with no libraries, or no pages}

A package with pages but no libraries, or with libraries but no pages, is
ordinary. The driver writes a landing page for every package that does not
ship an [index.mld], and one for every library, so every package has an entry
point. Modules whose names contain [__] are hidden: they are compiled, because
other modules depend on them, but not linked or rendered.

{3 Dependencies documented elsewhere}

By default every dependency is documented too, so that links into them work.
With [--remap], dependencies are compiled, because the selected packages'
units depend on their [.odoc] files, but not linked or rendered, and links
into them are rewritten to point at their documentation on ocaml.org.

{2:ocamlorg ocaml.org: one package per job}

ocaml.org documents every version of every package. Each is built by a
separate job that runs [odoc_driver_voodoo] on the output of [voodoo-prep]:
a directory [prep/universes/<u>/<pkg>/<version>] holding the files the package
installed. The dependencies are installed in the job's switch, and their
[.odoc] files are those earlier jobs wrote into the odoc directory, at the
locations the mirror layout predicts. The switch holds that package's closure
and no more, so findlib is often not there to be asked and the facts come from
the [META] files and the compiler, as {{!installed}step 1} describes.
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Voodoo}[Voodoo]} reads the prepared package
into a [Packages.t]; everything after that is the same code as for a switch,
applied to one package. That the two drivers share the compile step is not
tidiness: a package's own libraries require one another as often as libraries
of different packages do, and compiling them in whatever order they were
listed in leaves what the later ones should have expanded unresolved.
Identifiers start with [p/<pkg>/<version>], or [u/<universe>/<pkg>/<version>]
for a version that is not the one ocaml.org shows by default.

The job scheduler needs the compile and link phases separately, for the same
forward-reference reason as above: [--actions compile-only] compiles a
package, [--actions link-and-gen] links and renders it once the packages its
scope names are compiled. Occurrence counts and the JSON search index that
uses them are produced at the end of the link job.

{1:conv Convention for installed packages}

For drivers and installed packages to work together, both follow the
convention below. The reference driver implements it; other drivers should
give the same results.

{2 Trees and their roots}

Each installed package [<p>] defines one page tree, named [<p>], with root
identifier [<p>]. Each of its libraries [<l>] defines one module tree, named
[<l>], with root identifier [<p>/<l>].

A package [foo] with libraries [foo] and [foo.bar] thus defines three trees:
the page tree [foo], and the module trees [foo] rooted at [foo/foo] and
[foo.bar] rooted at [foo/foo.bar]. Another package refers to them with
[-P foo:<dir>], [-L foo:<dir>] and [-L foo.bar:<dir>] at link time, where each
[<dir>] holds the tree's [.odoc] files.

The driver may root the trees differently as long as references still
resolve; ocaml.org puts the version in the root identifier.

{2:pkg_units The units of a package}

The module units of a package are the compiled modules of the libraries it
installed below [lib]. The page units are the [.mld] files below
[doc/<p>/odoc-pages], and the [.md] files directly below [doc/<p>], which go
through [odoc-md] rather than [odoc compile]. The asset units are the other
files below [doc/<p>/odoc-pages], and all files below [doc/<p>/odoc-assets].

The driver adds pages of its own, which a package cannot supply:

- one for the package, unless it ships [doc/<p>/odoc-pages/index.mld], listing
  its pages and, per library, its modules;
- one for each library, listing that library's modules;
- one at [<p>/src], introducing the rendered sources, when any module of the
  package has an implementation to render.

Each is an [.mld] the driver writes to a scratch directory and compiles like
any other page. The library pages and the source page carry
[@toc_status hidden], which keeps them out of the table of contents, and
[@order_category], which decides where they sit among the package's pages.

{2 Parent ids}

Interface and implementation units have the root of their library tree as
parent id: [<p>/<l>].

A page found at [doc/<p>/odoc-pages/<relpath>/<name>.mld] has the parent id
[<p>/<relpath>]. An asset found there likewise. An asset found at
[doc/<p>/odoc-assets/<name>] has the parent id [<p>/_assets].

Implementation units have the source id [<p>/src/<l>/<name>.ml].

{2 The reference scope}

A package's units are linked with the scope described in the
{{!standard}walkthrough}: [-L] for its own libraries, the libraries they
require directly, and those named in [odoc-config.sexp]; [-P] for itself, the
packages providing those libraries, and those named in [odoc-config.sexp].
The transitive closure is deliberately not included. To refer to something in
a transitive dependency, name it in the [META] [requires] field or in
[odoc-config.sexp]. This is the driver side of the
{{!odoc_for_authors.reference_scope}reference scope} that authors see.

The dependency cone is a different matter: it must reach everything a unit's
modules depend on, transitively, because expansions and source links do. The
driver gives it to [odoc compile] as [-L], naming each library, and to
[odoc link] as [-I]. Odoc searches the recorded libraries, and then the [-I]
directories, before the rest of the scope, so where a name is in both a unit
gets the module it was built against; and because the names are written into
the units, that holds for another run resolving into them as well, not only
for the unit on the command line. The page the driver writes
for a library is given that library's cone for the same reason.

{2 [odoc-config.sexp]}

A package [<p>] may install [doc/<p>/odoc-config.sexp], holding
s-expressions:

- [(packages p1 p2 ...)] adds the page trees [p1], [p2], ... to the scope, and
  the module trees of the libraries those packages provide;
- [(libraries l1 l2 ...)] adds the module trees [l1], [l2], ... to the scope,
  and the page trees of the packages providing them.

{2 Ordering the generated pages}

The convention introduces directories, one per library, whose order in the
sidebar an author cannot set with [@children_order]. In the pages it writes
itself, the driver uses the [@order_category <name>] tag to help [odoc] sort
them. The resulting order is:

- first, pages in the order given by their parent's [@children_order];
- then by kind: pages and directories, then modules, then implementations,
  then everything else;
- then by [@order_category], compared as a string, a page that sets none
  compared as the string [default];
- within a category, by [@short_title] where the page sets one, otherwise by
  the page's own name, or for a page named [index] by the name of its
  directory;
- ties are left in the order the units were given.

[@order_category] may change and is not for authors to use. It is for the
pages a driver generates.
